ven on the domain $E$ if they are compatible.\n        Actually, the standard theorem states that for the spectral radius, if $E \\subseteq X$, then $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$ provided we define the spectral radius consistently.\n        However, the definition "spectral radius of $A$ restricted to $E$" is usually the *ordinary* spectral radius on the completion of $E$.\n        The question asks: "Is the spectral radius of $A$ on $X$ also equal to $r$?"\n        If $E$ is dense, $X$ is the completion of $E$.\n        The spectral radius on a Banach space is defined via the global spectrum.\n        So, is $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$?\n\n    *   **Refining the answer:**\n        Let\'s assume $E$ is a closed subspace for the sake of standard spectral theory discussion (often $E$ is assumed closed, but the problem states dense).\n        If $E$ is dense, does $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$ hold?\n        Let $\\sigma(A)$ be the set of $\\lambda$ for which $X(A-\\lambda I)$ has a null space.\n        Then $\\rho(A) = \\sup \\{|\\lambda| : \\lambda \\in \\sigma(A)\\}$.\n        $\\sigma(A|_E)$ is the spectrum of the restriction. $\\sigma(A|_E) = \\{ \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{C} : X(A - \\lambda I)_E \\neq 0 \\}$.\n        Since $E$ is dense, $\\sigma(A) = \\{ \\lambda \\in \\mathbb{C} : X(A - \\lambda I) \\neq 0 \\}$.\n        Note that $X(A - \\lambda I) = \\{ f \\in \\text{Ext}(X) : f(E) = \\epsilon \\lambda \\}$.\n        $X(A - \\lambda I) \\neq 0$ usually implies there is a neighborhood in $X(A - \\lambda I)$ that intersects $\\text{Ext}(E)$.\n        Actually, since $A|_E \\in S(E)$ (where $S(E)$ is the space of bounded operators on $E)$, by the Alon-Tarski theorem, the spectral radius of $A$ is the same as $A|_E$?\n        No, Alon-Tarski says the *spectral measure* on the algebra generated by $A$ on $E$ restricted to $E$ is the same. But for algebraic characteristic functions and spectral radius, it is often equal.\n\n        Let\'s look for the specific result: "Spectral radius of a bounded operator on a dense subspace."\n        Standard result: If $E \\subseteq X$ is dense in $X$ and $A$ is bounded on $X$ with domain $D(A) \\supseteq D(E)$ (or $A|_E$), then $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$.\n        Proof sketch:\n        The spectral radius $\\rho(A)$ is the supernatural number associated with the compactification of $E$.\n        It is known that spectral radius behaves "locally" on the closure.\n        Specifically, for any topological space $X$, and subspace $Y \\subseteq X$, $\\rho_{X,Y}(A) = \\rho_{X \\setminus Y}(A|_Y)$.\n        The usual spectral radius of an operator has a definition ( supernatural number) that depends only on the closure of the domain.\n        If $E$ is dense, the closure is $X$.\n        So we are comparing $\\rho(S(A))$ (where arg is spectral measure on the closure) and $\\rho(S(E))$ (where arg is spectral measure on the closure of $E$).\n        Actually, $S(E)_{closure}(A)$ is the spectral measure on the "thickened" completion of $E$.\n        The extended spectrum $\\{ \\lambda : S(E)(A - \\lambda I)_E \\neq 0 \\}$ is dense in $S(X)(A - \\lambda I)$ for $\\lambda$ with the same modulus provided the operator is pointwise null on a compact neighborhood in $X$.\n        Since $E$ is dense, the point-spaces $X$ are "small".\n        For any non-trivial null space of $A - \\lambda I$, its closure is a compact set of measure zero.\n        Wait, the spectral radius definition relies on the closure being discrete. The center IS discrete.\n        The point spectrum lies in the interior of the center? No, point spectrum lies in the center.\n        The center $Z(X)$ is finite-dimensional. The $1$-centralizer $C_1(Z(X))$ is a copy of $X$.\n        The spectrum is dense in the center.\n        We are looking for $\\sup \\{|\\lambda| : A - \\lambda I \\equiv 0\\}$.\n        If $E$ is dense, $\\sigma(A|_E)$ is dense in $\\sigma(A)$.\n        Is $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$?\n        This is not always true for spectrum *v