fectively, $\\rho(A) = \\rho(A|_E)$ should hold.\n\n    *   **Let\'s double check with a specific example where it might fail or hold.**\n        Let $X = \\ell^2^E$ (Unit ball in $\\mathbb{R}^\\mathbb{N}$). Wait, topology on $\\mathbb{R}^\\mathbb{N}$ is not first, but vector space is.\n        Elements are $x = (x_1, x_2, \\dots)$.\n        Let $A_1(k, x) = k x$. Spectrum $\\mathbb{N}$. $\\rho=1$.\n        Let $x^n = (0, \\dots, 0, 0, 1, 0)$. $A_1$ has null space.\n        Is it possible that $\\rho(A) > \\rho(A|_E)$?\n        No, if $\\lambda \\in \\sigma(A)$, then $\\bar{A}(x^n) \\rightharpoonup \\lambda x^n$.\n        Also $A(t x^n) = t (A x) x^n$.\n        If $\\lambda \\notin \\sigma(A|_E)$, then $\\bar{A}(t x^n) \\nrightarrow \\lambda t x^n$.\n        If $\\rho(A) = r$, there is a neighborhood $U$ of $m$ where $A - rI \\equiv 0$ (infinite syndrome matrix with continuum null space, i.e., infinite support null space directions).\n        Does $A - rI$ have this null space in $X$? Yes.\n        Then there are $x_n \\in E$ going to $x$ on $X$ such that $(A - rI)x_n = 0$ in the yawn (field of holomorphs).\n        If $\\rho(A|_E) = r$, then $A - rI$ has null space only in $E$ (weakly)? Or is it actually null?\n        If the field of holomorphs on $E$ is dense in that of $X$ (which it is), and in the yawn of the function on $E$, does it imply nullity in $X$?\n        The spectral radius $\\rho(A|_E)$ is the supremum of $|\\lambda|$ such that the yawn of $A(x)$ goes to $\\lambda x$.\n        If $\\rho(A|_E) = k$, then $\\sup \\{|\\lambda| : \\bar{A}(x) \\rightharpoonup \\lambda x\\} = k$.\n        This $\\sup$ is a characteristic on the natural numbers (or space of sequences).\n        It is known that spectral radius is a spectral characteristic (characteristic function $cx$) for the spectral measure.\n        Actually, spectral radius is *not* a spectral characteristic $cx$ in the strict algebraic sense (monotone class), unless the operator is self-adjoint or sim