ith the same f, we need A, B, C to be such that |A e^A| are equal. That forces two of them to be negative and one positive because otherwise, if all are negative, f(x) is increasing on negative side? Actually, on (-∞,0), f(x) = |x log_{2023} x| = |A e^A| (since e^A > 0, e is base? careful: In terms of A = ln x, we have f(x) = |e^A A|. On (-∞,0), A is negative, so e^A < 1, and A is negative, so product A e^A is negative. Its absolute value gives -A e^A. On (-∞,0), is the function |A e^A| monotonic? Consider φ(A)= -A e^A for A negative. As A → -∞, -A e^A → 0 (since e^A → 0 faster than A → ∞). As A increases from -∞ to 0, -A e^A increases to 0 at A=0? Actually, derivative: d/dA (-A e^A) = - (e^A(1+A)). For A<0, this is positive if 1+A < 0, i.e., A < -1, and negative if A > -1. So φ(A) decreases from 0 at A→ -∞ to a minimum at A=-1? Actually, if derivative is negative for A < -1, then φ decreases as A increases from -∞ to -1. Then for A > -1, derivative is positive, so φ increases. So φ(A) has a minimum at A=-1. So for a given value of φ(A) (with 0 < φ(A) < 1/e), there are two A: one in (-∞, -1) and one in (-1, 0). And for A > 0, φ(A) increases from 0 to ∞, so for any value, there is one A > 0. So indeed, three distinct A correspond to two in (-∞,0) with opposite sides of -1 and one in (0, ∞). So for a < b < c, we have A < B < C. Since a, b, c are positive and increasing, A, B, C are increasing. So it is possible that A and B are the two negative ones (with A < B < -1? But then C > 0 > B? That would give A, B negative, C positive. But if A < B < C, and C is positive, then we have A < B < C. But if B is negative and C positive, then they are not all negative. So we could have A < B < -1 and C > 0, or A < -1 < B < 0 and C > 0? But in our analysis, we had two negative numbers: one less than -1 and one greater than -1 but still negative. So if A and B are both negative, then A < -1 and B > -1? But then if A < B, it is possible that A < -1 and B > -1, so A < -1 < B < 0. And C > 0. That gives A < B < C. So that is consistent: A < -1, B ∈ (-1,0), C > 0. Then a = e^A < e^{-1}, b = e^B ∈ (e^{-1},1), c = e^C > 1. And we have geometric progression condition: 2B = A + C. So B is the geometric mean of A and C. Since A < -1, B > -1, but B could be negative, C positive. So we have A + C = 2B. Since A is negative, C is positive, 2B could be positive or negative depending on B. If B > 0.5, then A+C > 1, possible. If B < 0.5, then A+C < 1. But A is negative and C is positive, so it\'s possible.\n\nSo under geometric progression, we have A, B, C with A < -1, B ∈ (-1,0), C > 0, and 2B = A+C, and |A e^A| = |B e^B| = |C e^C|. That is exactly what we set up. And we derived equations that led to d=0. So maybe there is indeed no solution for geometric progression? But we know from the function that there are three distinct x with the same f(x) value. So they exist, but they might not be in geometric progression. So perhaps the intended condition is arithmetic progression. \n\nGiven the time, I\'ll try to solve under arithmetic progression condition: 2b = a + c. Let\'s use the variables u, v, w as before, with u = e^A, v = e^B, w = e^C, and u < e^{-1}, v ∈ (e^{-1},1), w > 1. And we have:\nu(-ln u) = v(-ln v) = w ln w = K,\nand 2v = u + w.\n\nWe want to find the common difference d = v - u.\n\nMaybe we can find a neat relation if we consider that the function φ(t) = t e^t is natural. Actually, note that |A e^A| = K/constant? I\'m mixing bases.\n\nLet\'s try to eliminate K by taking ratios. From u(-ln u) = v(-ln v), we have:\nln u = (K/u) + ln u? Not helpful.\n\nAlternatively, let\'s set u = e^{-1} - ε, v = e^{-1} + δ, with ε, δ > 0. Then w = 2v - u = 2(e^{-1}+δ) - (e^{-1}-ε) = e^{-1} + 2δ + ε.\nThen we have:\n(u(-ln u)) = (e^{-1}-ε)(1 - ln(1-ε e)) = (e^{-1}+δ)(1 + ln(1+δ e)) = (e^{-1}+2δ+ε) ln(e^{-1}+2δ+ε).\nThat is a system of two equations in ε and δ. They are transcendental, unlikely to yield a simple closed form. So the answer in the form k√m+n suggests