+b/3+c/5). So for given residues of N modulo 2,3,5, we compute RHS = 30*(a/2+b/3+c/5) and see if it equals N. Since N is of the form 30k + t, we have N = 30k + t. The RHS = 30*(a/2+b/3+c/5) is an integer? a/2 etc. are fractions. So RHS may not be integer. But L+R+S is integer. So N ≠ RHS. For each residue t (i.e., each combination of a,b,c), we can compute RHS. For t such that RHS is not an integer, then certainly N ≠ RHS for all N with that residue, because N is integer. But if RHS is an integer with the same residue as t, then it\'s possible that N = RHS for that residue class, i.e., there exists k such that 30k+t = RHS, which is 30*(a/2+b/3+c/5). That would mean that for all N with that residue, L+R+S = N. So we want residues where RHS is not an integer or where for all k, 30k+t ≠ RHS. But since RHS is a constant for given t, if RHS is an integer and congruent to t mod 30, then for N = RHS itself, we have N = RHS. But for other N with the same residue, N > RHS (if RHS is small). So if RHS is an integer and less than 30, then only for N = RHS exactly we have equality. But if RHS is greater than 30, then maybe there are multiple N in that residue class that equal RHS? But RHS is a constant for each residue class, so only one N in that residue class equals RHS if RHS is positive and less than some bound. Actually, for fixed t, RHS = 30*(a/2+b/3+c/5) is a fixed number. Then the equation N = 30k+t = RHS means that t = RHS mod 30, and if RHS mod 30 = t, then RHS is of the form 30k+t for some k. Actually, RHS might not be a multiple of 30, so it\'s not of the form 30k+t directly. But if we write RHS = 30k + t, then k = (RHS - t)/30 must be integer. So for given t, there may or may be an integer k such that 30k+t = RHS. That gives a specific N. So for that N, L+R+S = N. For other N with the same residue t, N is different, so likely L+R+S ≠ N. So the condition for Baron to be true is that his N is not that specific N that makes L+R+S = N. But we want the largest N that could have been in the crowd such that Baron\'s statement is true. That means we want the largest N for which, under the rounding rule Baron uses, L+R+S ≠ N. Since for each residue class except the ones that make RHS an integer equal to some N in that class, Baron can choose any N in that class (except possibly one) and his statement will be true. But if there is exactly one N in a residue class that makes L+R+S = N, then for all other N in that class, L+R+S ≠ N, so Baron could take that N. But if there is no N in that class that makes L+R+S = N, then Baron can take any N in that class. Since residues mod 30 produce infinite N, the largest N is infinite. So we need to consider if there is any largest N that works? Possibly there is an upper bound because betrayal might occur? But the Baron never lies, so his statement is true for his N. There is no other constraint.\n\nMaybe the problem is asking: Given that Baron\'s statement is true (the sum is not equal to one), what is the largest possible N that could make that statement true? But as we argued, for any N not equal to a specific values that cause L+R+S = N, the statement is true. So there is no maximum; we can take arbitrarily large N. But maybe we need to consider that Baron\'s rounding is used to explain why the fractions sum to more than 1 but rounding makes it not equal to 1. But the Duke says the sum isn\'t equal to one, Baron says it\'s rounding. That implies that the sum of the rounded numbers is not N. But if we take rounding rules that round down for the overfat fractions to compensate, the sum could be N. So Baron wants it not equal to N. So maybe we want the largest N such that no matter how you round (with the usual rounding rule), the sum is not N. But as we computed, for residues 0,5,9,13 mod 30, sum equals N. But for other residues, sum is not N. So there are infinitely many N. Unless we impose that Baron uses a specific rounding rule, and we want the largest N for which his statement is true regard