dit card loan if it earns interest).\n\nBut we need to incorporate interest on the credit card loan. If the credit card loan earns interest, then paying early minimizes interest cost. That could be significant. But the interest rate on the credit card loan is not given. Possibly we assume that the credit card interest rate is also 6% per year? That is plausible if they use the same bank. But then why mention debit card interest rate? Maybe to indicate that there is an existing debit account that could earn interest if kept intact. Possibly the credit card loan might earn interest at a higher rate, but not specified. Alternatively, the problem might be designed to compare the cashback differences and the interest on the debit account after spending vs. not spending.\n\nLet\'s think from the perspective of the ultimate beneficiary: The buyer of the tickets. He starts with a certain amount of money (either in debit account or separate funds). When he uses debit card, he spends 20000, so his total wealth decreases by 20000, but then he earns interest on the remaining balance and gets 200 cashback (which might be deposited in his account or given as cash, so net destruction of wealth). When he uses credit card, he does not spend his own funds; he just borrows 20000 and then repays it. So his net wealth is unchanged. But he gets 100 cashback (lower than debit). So credit gives less benefit. So why would credit be more profitable? Possibly because in debit option, his debit account balance might be lower than if he kept the funds idle? Actually, if he uses debit card and spends 20000 from an account that could earn interest, he reduces the interest-earning capacity. If he uses credit card, he doesn\'t touch his debit account, so his debit account earns interest on the full amount. That could be an advantage. So we need to compare the final balance in the debit account for both scenarios.\n\nScenario 1 (Debit): Debit account initial balance B0. After purchase: balance = B0 - 20000. Interest earned over month: I = 0.005*(B0 - 20000) (using monthly rate 0.005). Cashback 200 added at end. Final debit balance = (B0 - 20000) + 0.005*(B0 - 20000) + 200 = 1.005B0 - 20000 - 100 + 200 = 1.005B0 - 19900.\n\nScenario 2 (Credit): If we repay within N days (assuming N <= 30), we pay back 20000 from credit card, so credit card balance goes to 0. We don\'t touch debit account, so debit account remains B0. Cashback 100 is added to credit card account. So debit account remains unchanged = B0. However, if we repay later (after N days) with penalty, debit account still remains B0, but we might have to pay penalty to credit card. But the problem only asks for N such that paying within N days is more profitable. So for N large enough that paying within N days yields better outcome, we assume we repay within N days. So we compare: For credit card, final debit account = B0 (since we never spend from debit account). Cashback is separate, but maybe we need to compare total situation? Could it be that the problem considers only the debit account balance? Possibly the comparative benefit is the difference in empowerment: In debit card, we end up with less because we spent money; in credit card, we end up with more because we didn\'t spend. So we want to find N such that not spending (like using credit card with early repayment) yields better final debit balance than spending. But then why the debit card interest rate? It matters: Spending from debit reduces the balance that earns interest. So we want to find N such that the benefit of early repayment (avoiding reduction of debit balance) outweighs the cashback difference and the opportunity cost of having lower balance when earning interest.\n\nIf we don\'t spend (i.e., we use credit card and repay early), our debit balance remains B0, and we earn interest on B0 throughout the month. If we spend (debit card), our debit balance decreases to B0 - 20000, and we earn interest on that smaller amount. So the differen