terest on the balance I spend. So I need to compare the actual benefit I get from buying airline tickets differently.\n\nBuying airline tickets: The cost is 20,000 rubles. If I use debit card, I pay 20,000, and receive 0.01 cashback after a month, plus interest on the reduced balance. If I use credit card and repay early, I pay 20,000 borrowed, get 0.005 cashback, and I avoid deducting 20,000 from my debit account. So effectively, using credit card avoids the expense of reducing the debit account balance.\n\nBut the benefit of avoiding reducing the debit account balance is the interest that would have been earned on that balance if I had kept it. So I need to compute how much I gain or lose by choosing credit card vs debit card, relative to not spending the money at all? Actually, if I don\'t spend the money, my debit account balance stays at B0, and I earn interest on that balance throughout the month. If I spend, I earn interest on (B0 - 20000). The difference is 20000 * 0.005 = 100 rubles more. But when I spend (debit card), I also get 1% cashback instead of 0.5%. So overall, the debit card option gives me the interest difference (100) plus cashback difference (-100). That totals 0. So from a cash and interest perspective, the debit card and credit card (with early repayment) seem equivalent? That seems odd. But wait: In credit card option, if I repay early, I also have the cashback of 0.5%, which is lower than debit card\'s 1%. So overall, debit card gives 1% cashback + 100 interest surplus, credit card gives 0.5% cashback + 0 interest surplus (since I keep the balance). So debit card gives a net gain of 400 in cash+interest, while credit card gives no gain. So credit card is worse. That would mean debit card is always better. But the problem asks for N such that credit card is more profitable. So maybe we misinterpret cashback: Possibly cashback is given only if you use the card for spending, but it might be given as an extra cash, not credited to your account.