D*(1+i_monthly) - C*(1 - i_monthly) + C*r_d).\nOption Credit: With early repayment:\nWe have loan of C from credit. We repay early: Payoff = C. So credit card balance returns to 0. Debit balance remains D. Cashback = C * r_c = C*r_c credited to credit card account. So final amounts: Debit = D, Credit = C*r_c.\nIf we don\'t repay early (i.e., repay later with penalty), debit balance still remains D, credit card balance might earn interest? But if credit card earns interest, then repay later would incur interest cost. But penalty might be equivalent to some extra interest. The problem does not specify penalty, so likely we assume that if we repay within N days, we avoid any interest/penalty cost. So for comparison, we assume we repay within N days.\n\nNow we want to determine when credit card is more profitable than debit card. Profit could be measured as the final amount of money in the debit account (since debit account is the main account with interest) or as the total wealth across accounts. But as we saw, if cashback goes to debit account, then debit total is higher for credit option; if cashback goes to credit account, then comparing only debit account gives deficit for credit option. So we need to figure out the intended measure.\n\nPerhaps the problem considers the "profit" as the net benefit from using credit card compared to debit card, maybe factoring in that using credit card might involve interest on the loan if not repaid early, but if repaid early, no interest. But also, if you use credit card, you are using a credit card to make the purchase, so you are effectively borrowing. Borrowing with credit card typically involves interest rate. So let’s assume that credit card loan earns interest at some rate. But the problem only mentions debit card interest rate. Could it be that credit card interest is higher? Possibly, but not given. Perhaps the interest rate on credit card is also 6% per year? That would be typical for credit cards, higher than debit. But 