ial debit balance. Typically, B0 might be small, so credit could be better if B0 is low. But the problem asks for N, so N influences B0? No, N is the repayment deadline for credit card. So N doesn\'t affect B0.\n\nMaybe we consider total wealth across both accounts: D_final_c + C_final_c = B0 + 100. D_final_d + C? In debit, what is the credit card final? In debit option, we do not use credit card, so credit card balance is unchanged (assuming we didn\'t open it), so C_final_d = 0? Then total debit + credit = D_final_d + 0 = 1.005B0 - 19900. So credit total vs debit total: B0 + 100 vs 1.005B0 - 19900. Credit total > debit total if B0 + 100 > 1.005B0 - 19900 => 100 + 19900 > 1.005B0 - B0 => 20000 > 0.005B0 => B0 < 20000/0.005 = 4,000,000. So for B0 < 4 million, credit total is higher. So credit is more profitable if initial debit balance is less than 4 million rubles. That is a plausible scenario if we hold money in an account rather than spend it. But then why does N come into play? N is the number of days within which you must repay the credit card payment. In credit option, we assumed we repay within N days, but N doesn\'t affect the total if we repay early. So total wealth is B0 + 100, independent of N. So if B0 < 4 million, credit is better regardless of N. That answer would be trivial N=1? But the problem asks for minimum N, implying N depends on something.\n\nMaybe N affects the credit card interest if we repay after N days. Because if we repay after N days, we might incur extra charges, possibly interest on the credit card balance. So the credit card option might involve paying interest if we don\'t repay within N days. So we need to consider the best time to repay to maximize profit. That is a typical problem: you have a purchase, you choose between debit and credit, and you want to repay the credit card as soon as possible to avoid paying interest, but you also have cashback. So the decision is: when should you repay the credit card to maximize your net bene