interest on that 20000 for the time you kept it idle. So if you repay early, you earn interest on B for the full period; if you repay late, you still earn interest on B for the full period, but you also pay the overdue penalty, which is the interest you would have lost if you had kept the 20000 in the debit account. So the benefit of repaying early is that you earn the debit interest on the remaining balance instead of the debit interest on the full balance. That benefit is exactly the interest you would have earned on 20000 if you had kept it in the account. So the difference in interest earned is 20000 * r. So by repaying early (within N days), you avoid that 20000 * r interest. Meanwhile, you lose 500 in cashback. So the net advantage of repaying early is 20000*r - 500. If 20000*r > 500, then repaying early is advantageous. With r = 0.005, 20000*0.005 = 100, which is less than 500. So 20000*r - 500 = 100 - 500 = -400. So repaying early gives a net loss of 400. So it would never be profitable to repaying early if we consider only interest lost on 20000 and cashback difference. But wait, when we use credit card, we also keep the money in the debit account, so we earn interest on B for 30 days. On the debit option, we earn interest on (B - 20000) for 30 days. So the difference in interest earned is exactly 20000*r. That is always 100. So if we get 100 back as cashback in debit, we lose 500 in cashback, net loss 400. So debit always better in terms of these two factors. So why would credit be better? Possibly because the cashback in credit is not just a small amount; maybe the cashback is not credited to your account but is a rebate that reduces your effective cost? If cashback is a rebate on the purchase, then using debit you pay 20000 and get 200 back later, so net cost 19980. Using credit, you pay 20000 and get 100 back, so net cost 19900. So credit is better by 80 immediately. But then you also have the debit interest difference. If you realize the 80 benefit from credit, then you also have the debit interest difference: debit gives you interest on (B-20000), credit gives you interest on B. So the total expected benefit from credit is 80 + 20000*r = 80 + 100 = 180. From debit, it\'s -80 - 20000*r = -80 - 100 = -180. So debit gives net 180, credit gives net -180. So debit is better. So debit seems always better.\n\nGiven that, maybe the intended interpretation is that the cashback is not received as an extra payment but as a discount on the purchase? For example, if you use debit card, you pay 20000 but you get 200 back at the end, effectively costing 19800. With credit card, you pay 20000 but you get 100 back, effectively costing 19900. So credit costs more. That would mean debit is better. So debit always better.\n\nMaybe the problem is about the timing of cashback: With debit card, cashback is received after 1 month, but you also have the benefit of interest on the remaining balance. With credit card, you get cashback after 1 month but you avoid reducing your debit balance. The comparison should be based on the distinction: Using credit card allows you to not lose money in your debit account. So if you hold the money in your debit account, you earn interest on it. If you spend it, you don\'t earn interest on that amount. So the opportunity cost of spending is the interest forgone. That forgone interest is 100. But you also get less cashback (400 less). So it\'s never beneficial to spend. So N doesn\'t matter.\n\nGiven that the problem asks for N, I think there must be something about the repayment of the credit card. Possibly N is the grace period, and if you repay within N days, you pay no extra charges, but if you repay after N days, you pay extra charges that are proportional to the number of days. Then we need to find N such that the extra charges for late repayment are less than the benefit of repaying early. That would determine when it is more profitable to use credit card. So we need to compute the extra charges if you repay after N days. Possibly the extra charges are a fixed amount per day or an interest rate. Since not specified, maybe we assume that the extra charges are equal to the charges that would have been charged for the money you spent? That is, if you repay after N days, you lose the interest you would have earned on the 20000 if you had kept it in the account. So the penalty is 20000 * (0.005/30) * (30 - N) = 20000 * r * (30 - N) with r = 0.005. That is a plausible assumption: If you repay the credit card loan after N days, you pay a penalty equal to the interest you would have earned on your own funds for the number of days overdue. So if you repay within N days, you avoid that payment. So then we compare the net benefit of repaying within N days.\n\nNow, for debit option, you spend 20000 and pay 20000 cashback after 30 days. For credit option, you pay 20000 early (within N days) or late (after N days), and you get 100 cashback after 30 days.\n\nLet\'s compute the net benefit of each option considering the replacement of debit spending.\n\nWe consider the money you have at time 0. Let it be some amount M (could be the debit balance, but we\'ll just consider your cash). Option Debit:\n- You spend 20000 from your money (you give 20000 to debit). After spending, you still have M - 20000 cash? Actually, if you have M cash, spending 20000 reduces cash to M - 20000. But you also have a debit account balance that contains M? Actually, the problem says you have sufficient funds available, so maybe your funds are separate from the debit account. When you use debit card, you withdraw 20000 from your funds and spend from debit account. When you use credit card, you instead borrow 20000 from the credit card and repay later (or early). So we need to consider the net effect on your disposable income.\n\nAssume you have a certain amount of money that you can use to buy the tickets. Let that amount be B0. When you use debit card, you spend 20000 from your money, so you have B0 - 20000 left. But you also earn interest on the debit account for the month. If you use credit card and repay early, you borrow 20000 from credit card, so your personal funds remain B0, and you repay 20000 later, so after repayment, your personal funds return to B0. Then you get cashback of 100, which is added to credit card account, but not necessarily to personal funds. If cashback is added to personal funds, then your personal funds become B0 + 100 after 30 days. If not, they remain B0. To compare, we likely add personal funds only to where the cashback goes. So if cashback is given to your personal account with debit card, then personal funds become (B0 - 20000) + interest + 200 = B0*(1.005) - 19900. If cashback is given to your personal account with credit card, then personal funds become B0 + (100 - 20000?) Actually, if cashback is given as an extra payment, it could be considered as reducing the cost: Using debit costs you 20000 with 200 back after 30 days, so effective cost 19980. Using credit costs you 20000 with 100 back after 30 days, effective cost 19900. So credit is better by 80 immediately. That is independent of interest. So if cashback is considered a rebate, credit is always better. But then interest shouldn\'t matter. So likely cashback is added to your account.\n\nGiven the confusion, I think the intended problem is to compare the net effect on the debit account only. That is, we assume that cashback is added to the debit account. So debit cashback goes to debit account, credit card cashback goes to credit card account (not debit). Then at the end of the month, we compare the remaining balance in our debit account after both options. And we also consider the time to repay credit card loan. For debit, there is no repayment date, just cashback. For credit, we have to repay within N days to get good interest on credit card. But if we repay within N days, we pay both the debit interest and credit card cashback and the reduced debit balance. If we repay after N days, we incur extra charges on credit card.\n\nSo we need to compute the final debit balance for both options, and also account for any extra charges on credit card if repaid after N days.\n\nLet’s define:\nLet the initial debit account balance be D. When we make the purchase, we consider that we are paying for the airline tickets, so we want to minimize the cost. The cost is the amount we pay now plus future costs.\n\nOption Debit:\n- Pay D - 20000 from debit to buy tickets.\n- After purchase, debit balance becomes D - 20000.\n- During 30 days, interest earned on debit: I = r*(D - 20000).\n- Cashback of 200 added to debit account.\n- Final debit balance: D_final_d = (D - 20000) + r*(D - 20000) + 200 = D(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200.\n\nOption Credit: Two subcases: repay within N days or after N days.\nSubcase A: Repay within N days (N <= current day, but we consider repayment after paying, so we work backwards). Assume we decide to repay within N days to avoid extra charges. So we pay 20000 from credit card at some day before N. Cashback at end of month.\n- We borrow 20000 from credit card.\n- Debit balance remains D (unchanged).\n- Cashback of 100 added to credit account.\n- Final debit balance: D_final_c1 = D.\n- Credit balance becomes 0 (since we repaid).\n- Cashback credited at end of month: credit account gets 100.\nSo total wealth after option: debit + credit = D + 100.\n\nSubcase B: Repay after N days: we repay 20000 on day 30 (end of month), but we incur extra charges for being overdue. What are these extra charges? Possibly they are the interest we would have earned on our own funds for the allowed grace period, or they are a penalty based on the overdraft period. Since the problem says "stay within the grace period" implies that within N days there is no extra charge, but after that there is an extra charge. The extra charge likely has to be someone to decide. Assume that the extra charge is a fixed amount per day or a percentage of the principal? Not given. Could it be that the extra charges are exactly the amount that would have been credited to our debit account for the days we saved money? That is, if we repay after N days, we lose the opportunity to earn interest on our money for N days. So the extra charge equals that lost interest. Then if we repay after N days, we pay the principal plus the interest that we would have earned on that principal if we had kept it in the debit account. So the total amount we pay in credit card when repaying after N days is 20000 + 20000 * r * (30 - N). We also get cashback of 100.\n- Debit balance remains D.\n- Credit balance goes from 20000 to 20000 * (1 + r * (30 - N)) (if credit earns interest at same rate r). But wait, if we repay after N days, we might close the credit card or not? Usually, you keep the credit card open and pay interest, but if you pay the full balance, there is no interest. So it\'s more likely that if you repay after N days, you pay the principal but also pay interest for the overdue period. So credit balance earns interest. So credit final balance: C_final_c2 = 20000 + 20000 * r * (30 - N) (if credit interest rate is r).\n- Cashback of 100 added to credit account.\nSo total wealth after option: debit + credit = D + 20000 * (1 + r * (30 - N)) + 100.\n\nNow compare option Debit total = D(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200 = D(1+r) - 20000 + 200r + 200.\nOption Credit within N: D + 100.\nOption Credit after N: D + 20000 + 20000*r*(30 - N) + 100 = D + 20000(1 + r*(30 - N)) + 100.\n\nWe want to determine when credit option is more profitable than debit option. That could be for repayment within N days or after N days. The problem asks: "Determine the minimum number of days N, under which all other conditions being equal, it is more profitable to pay for the purchase with the credit card." So we need to find N such that credit option is more profitable than debit option. That would depend on the comparison between credit option (with any repayment time? but we choose to repay within N to avoid extra charges) and debit option. But note: If we repay after N days, that might be less profitable than repaying within N days, because extra charges are higher. So the optimal is to repay within N days if possible. So for the scenarios above, the optimal is to repay within N days, because that avoids extra charges and gives the credit card cashback. So we compare credit within N vs debit.\n\nSet credit within N total > debit total:\nD + 100 > D(1+r) - 20000 + 200r + 200.\nSimplify:\nD + 100 > D + D*r - 20000 + 200r + 200.\nCancel D from both sides:\n100 > D*r - 20000 + 200r + 200.\nRearrange:\n100 + 20000 - 200r < D*r.\n20000 + 100 - 200r < D*r.\nD*r > 20100 - 200r.\nWith r = 0.005:\n200r = 1.\nSo D*r > 20100 - 1 = 20099.\nSo D*r > 20099 => D > 20099 / 0.005 = 4,019,600 /0.005? 20099/0.005 = 4,019,600. That is a huge number. For D large, it holds; for D small, it doesn\'t. So credit within N is more profitable only if D is more than 4 million rubles. That seems unrealistic.\n\nMaybe we consider that the right-hand side should be compared differently. Let\'s plug numbers: r=0.005, so D*r is very small unless D is huge. Actually, D*r is small, so the inequality D*r > 20099 implies D is very large. So credit combination is more profitable only if you have a very large initial balance, which is unlikely. So credit within N is never more profitable? That suggests debit is always better. But the problem asks for N, so there must be something.\n\nMaybe we miscomputed debit total. Actually, debit total includes debit account and credit card? But for debit option, credit card is unchanged, so credit card final = 0 (assuming we didn\'t open credit card). So total D + C? For debit, C=0, so debit total = D_final_d. For credit within N, total = D + 100. Compare: D + 100 > D_final_d. We had D_final_d = D(1+r) - 20000 + 200r + 200. That gave D > 20099/r. So if D is large, debit is larger. So credit is always worse? That seems odd.\n\nMaybe we should compare only the debit account final balance, ignoring the credit card. For debit, credit card is not considered, so compare debit final balances? That would be trivial: forget credit cashback.\n\nAlternatively, maybe we compare the net cost of purchasing the ticket: The cost of purchasing is the amount paid now plus future losses. For debit, cost = 20000 now minus 200 cashback later, but also minus interest earned on debit for the remaining balance. So net cost = 20000 - [interest + cashback] on debit. For credit, cost = 20000 (principal) - 100 cashback, but also minus interest earned on credit card if kept open. So we need to incorporate that.\n\nLet’s try to compute the net cost of purchasing in terms of foregone opportunity.\n\nDefine: You have a debit account with balance D. You need to spend 20000. The opportunity cost of spending from your account is the foregone interest on that balance until 30 days.\n\nIf you spend 20000 from your account, your account balance goes down, so you earn less interest. The difference in interest is 20000*r (because if you spend, you lose interest on 20000; if you don\'t spend, you earn interest on 20000 for the full 30 days). Also, you also get cashback.\n\nSo if you spend (debit), you pay 20000 now, and you get 1% cashback later, and you lose 20000*r interest compared to if you didn\'t spend. So your net cost (in terms of foregone interest) is 20000 - r*20000 - 20000? That is 20000 - 100 - 20000 = -20000 -100? That\'s negative, so you gain net.\n\nIf you borrow (credit) and repay within N days, you pay 20000 now, you get 0.5% cashback, and you also don\'t reduce your account balance for the full period, so you earn interest on D for 30 days. But you also might have to repay within N days, so you get the credit card cashback. So your net gain is: you end up with credit card cashback and you keep D. Compared to debit: you end with debit account balance being reduced by 20000. The difference is the foregone interest on 20000 and the cashback difference.\n\nSo the net benefit of using credit compared to debit is: (interest earned on D) + (cashback difference) + (interest lost on 20000). So benefit = r*D + (100 - 200) + (-r*20000) = r*D - 100 - 20000*r = r*(D - 20000) - 100. For this to be positive, r*(D - 20000) > 100. With r=0.005, this requires D > 20000/0.005 + 100 = 4,000,000 + 100 = 4,000,100. So D must be greater than 4 million. Again, unrealistic.\n\nMaybe the cashback is not an extra payment but a rebate, so it reduces the net cost. Then the net cost of debit is 20000 - cashback = 20000 - 200 = 19980 (ignoring interest) and you also have foregone interest -r*20000 = -100, so total expected net cost = 19980 + 100 = 20080. For credit, net cost = 20000 - cashback = 20000 - 100 = 19900, but also you have foregone interest on D? Actually, with credit, you don\'t reduce D, so you don\'t lose interest, so no foregone interest cost. So expected net cost = 19900. So credit is better by 90. That is always better, independent of D. So then N doesn\'t matter.\n\nGiven the problem is worth 15 points, it\'s likely a short problem so it should have a simple answer. So maybe I\'m overcomplicating.\n\nLet\'s try reading the problem again: "Determine the minimum number of days $\\mathrm{N}$, under which all other conditions being equal, it is more profitable to pay for the purchase with the credit card." So N is the number of days you must repay to stay in grace period. The other conditions are: You have two cards, debit and credit. You buy tickets worth 20,000 rubles. With debit, you repay within N days to stay in grace period? Actually with debit you don\'t repay, just get cashback. With credit, you repay within N days to stay in grace period. The grace period likely means if you repay within N days, you pay the principal only and avoid extra charges; if you repay after N days, you pay extra charges. The extra charges are not quantified, so we must assume something. Usually, the extra charges could be the penalty for late payment on a credit card, which might be a percentage of the outstanding balance or a flat fee. But since not specified, maybe the problem expects us to assume that the extra charges are exactly the amount that would have been credited to your debit account for the period you kept the funds idle. That is, if you repay the credit card after N days, you lose interest on the 20000 for the number of days you saved it in the account. So the extra charge is 20000 * r * (30 - N). Alternatively, it could be interest at the end of the day. Since we need to include N in the answer, this assumption makes sense.\n\nSo we assume: If you repay the credit card within N days, you pay only 20000. If you repay after N days, you pay an extra charge, which is the amount that would have been credited to your debit account for the overdue period (i.e., the missing interest on your own money). So the total payment if you repay after N days is 20000 + 20000 * r * (30 - N). That yields a simple model.\n\nNow we compare the net outcome of using credit card versus debit card, considering that with credit card you can choose to repay within N days (so you get the credit card cashback and avoid extra charges) or repay after N days (so you incur extra charges). The better choice is to repay within N days if that gives you higher net profit. So we need to find N such that paying within N days (i.e., getting the deb advantage) is more profitable than not paying within N days (i.e., paying after N days). That is, we compare two contrasts: Option 1: Use credit card and repay within N days. Option 2: Use debit card. Option 3: Use credit card and repay after N days (with extra charges). Option 1 vs Option 2 determines when using credit card within N days is more profitable than using debit card. Options 1 and 3 are only relevant if N is small? Actually, if N is large enough that repaying within N days is always possible, then Option 1 is the best credit card option. The problem asks for the minimum N such that it is more profitable to use credit card. That likely means: For a given N, is Option 1 better than Option 2? And we find the threshold N where Option 1 becomes better.\n\nSo set up:\nOption Debit:\n- Pay 20000 now from debit.\n- After 30 days, get cashback 200.\n- Debit account earns interest: r*(B - 20000) on (B - 20000) for 30 days.\n- Cashback adds 200 to debit account.\nNet gain from debit: Interest = r*(B - 20000), cashback = 200, so total additional funds = r*(B - 20000) + 200. But you paid 20000, so net change in your wealth? If we track the balance in your wealth, if you use debit, your wealth at end = initial balance - 20000 + r*(B - 20000) + 200 = B(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200.\n\nOption Credit within N:\n- Borrow 20000 from credit card.\n- Repay 20000 within N days (so no extra charges).\n- Get credit card cashback 100 added to credit account.\n- Debit account remains B.\n- Cashback adds 100 to credit account, but is that added to your wealth? If it\'s added to credit account, it\'s not in your wealth. So if we consider your wealth as your own funds plus any non-interest-bearing cashback, then your wealth ends = B + 200 (credit card cashback if considered cash). But careful: The cashback from debit card is credited to debit account, so it\'s part of your wealth. Cashback from credit card is credited to credit card account, so if cashback is not transferred to your personal account, it is not part of your wealth. So if cashback is only available in the card account, then using debit card gives you cashback in your debit account, but using credit card gives you cashback in credit card account. So your wealth is only what\'s in your personal funds: after debit option, your personal funds = B(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200. After credit option (within N), your personal funds = B (since you didn\'t spend from personal funds). So the difference in wealth is: debit wealth - credit wealth = B(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200 - B = B*r - 20000(1 - r) + 200. As computed earlier, debit wealth is larger if B*r > 20000 - 200 + 200r = 20000 + 200r - 200 = 20100 + 200r. With r=0.005, that\'s >20110. So debit wealth is larger for moderate B. But if B is very high, debit could be smaller. So that doesn\'t give a threshold.\n\nMaybe we consider that the cashback from credit card is not available because it\'s given to the card account, but you might be able to convert it to personal wealth? Possibly not.\n\nGiven the confusion, maybe the intended analysis is simply: Using credit card allows you to not reduce your debit balance, so you earn interest on the full amount. Using debit reduces your debit balance and you get higher cashback. The time to repay the credit card loan affects whether you can keep the money in the debit account and earn interest on it. So if you repay the credit card loan quickly, you can both get credit card cashback and keep the money in debit to earn interest. So the benefit of repaying quickly is that you earn interest on the full amount instead of the amount reduced. But you get less cashback. So the net benefit of paying within N days is that you avoid reducing the debit balance for the time until 30 days. If you repay earlier, you keep the full amount for longer. The more you repay early, the greater the interest benefit. So if you repay within N days, you avoid reducing the debit balance for (30 - N) days. That amount you would have earned in debit interest. So the benefit of repaying early is r*20000*(30 - N). The cost of repaying early is that you get less cashback (400 less). So repaying early is beneficial if r*20000*(30 - N) > 400. Solve for N:\n0.005 * 20000 * (30 - N) > 400\n0.005 * 40000 * (30 - N) > 400\n200 * (30 - N) > 400  (since 0.005*40000=200)\n30 - N > 400/200 = 2\nN < 28 days.\nSo if you repay within 28 days, the interest benefit from keeping the full amount for longer exceeds the cashback difference. If you repay after 28 days, the interest benefit from keeping the full amount for the full 30 days is 100, which is less than 400. So it\'s beneficial to repay early as long as N < 28. That yields minimum N such that it is more profitable to pay within N days? Actually, if N=28, then interest benefit = r*20000*(30-28)=200*2=400, exactly equal to cashback difference. So for N > 28, the interest benefit is less than 400, so repaying within N days is less beneficial. But the problem asks for minimum N such that it is more profitable to pay with credit card. That would be N=1, because for any N, if you repay within N days, you are paying within the grace period, so no extra charges. But the comparison is between repaying within N days vs not repaying at all? Actually, if you use credit card, you always have to repay eventually. So we compare two strategies: using debit card and using credit card with repayment within N days. The repayment within N days gives you the benefit of low cashback and opportunity to keep the money in debit balance. The benefit of keeping the full amount is r*20000*(30 - N) days. So if N is small, keeping full amount for longer gives higher interest benefit. So for any small N, repaying early is beneficial compared to cashing out the money immediately? But if you use debit card, you don\'t have any constraint on repayment; you just get cashback. So we need to compare the net effect of debit vs credit with early repayment. But as we computed earlier, debit net gives B(1+r) - 20000(1-r) + 200, credit net gives B + 100. For N=1, credit net = B + 100, debit net = B(1+r) - 20000 + 20000r + 200. The difference is B(1+r) - B - 20000 + 20000r + 200 - 100 = B*r - 20000 + 10000r. With r=0.005, B*r - 20000 + 50 = B*0.005 - 19950. For B=4,000,000, B*r=20,000, so difference = 20,000 - 19,950 = 50 >0, so debit > credit. For B=1,000,000, difference = 5000 - 19,950 = -14,450, so debit < credit. So there is a threshold B where debit becomes better. But B is the initial debit balance, not given. Possibly we assume B is the amount you have, and we want to find N such that for any B, credit is better? That would require no B, which is impossible.\n\nGiven that the problem likely has a numeric answer (minimum number of days), I\'m leaning towards the interpretation that we are comparing the cashback and the debit interest difference, with N affecting the optimal timing of repayment. And the answer is N such that the interest forgone from reducing the debit balance by 20000 for the remaining days outweighs the debit card interest gain? That doesn\'t make sense.\n\nMaybe the problem is simpler: You have to choose to pay with debit or credit card. With debit, you repay within N days? Actually, the problem says: "pay for the purchase with the credit card (the credit limit allows it), you will have to repay the bank within N days to stay within the grace period." So N is the number of days you must repay if you use credit card. With debit card, you don\'t need to repay, you just get cashback after 1 month. So we compare the total cost of each option over the period, considering that for credit card, you have to repay within N days, and if you don\'t, you incur extra charges. But extra charges are not quantified, so maybe we assume that if you repay after N days, you pay an extra amount equal to the interest you would have earned on your own money for the time you kept it idle. That is, if you repay after N days, you pay the principal plus interest for (30 - N) days at rate r. Then we compare the two options for a given N, and find N such that credit option is better.\n\nLet’s do that with the assumption that extra charges for late repayment are proportional to the amount of interest on your own funds for the overdue period.\n\nSo:\nOption Debit:\n- Pay 20000 now.\n- After 30 days, get cashback 200.\n- Debit interest earned: r*(B - 20000) (since balance decreases to B-20000).\nSo total net gain from debit option: You end with B(1+r) - 20000(1 - r) + 200 = B + B*r - 20000 + 200r + 200.\n\nOpt