e different. So let BD = u, DA = v, AB = 10. For △AEC, let AE = p, EC = q, AC = 10. Congruence implies that there is a bijection between vertices such that the side lengths match. There are several possible correspondences. Given the symmetry, it is likely that AB corresponds to AC, so u = p or u = q, and v = q or v = p. Also, BD might correspond to CF or AE, etc.\n\nWe have additional information: DE = 6. D is on AE, so AE = AD + DE = v + 6. So p = v + 6.\nE is on CF, so CF = CE + EF. But we don\'t know CE or EF.\nAlso, F is on BD, so BD = BF + FD, but we don\'t know BF and FD.\nThe triangles △AEC, △CFB, and △BDA are all congruent. So we have three sets of lengths: (u, v, 10), (p, q, 10), (r, s, 10). Congruence means that these three triangles have the same side lengths in some order. So the set {u, v, 10} equals the set {p, q, 10} and also equals {r, s, 10}. So either u = p, v = q, and 10 = 10; or u = q, v = p, and 10 = 10; or u = r, etc. But it\'s also possible that one side of one triangle equals a side of another triangle, but not necessarily the same correspondence. Since they are triangles, they are congruent, so there is a specific vertex correspondence. Without loss of generality, we can assume a correspondence that makes the system solvable.\n\nGiven the symmetry of the equilateral triangle, it is plausible that the triangles are placed such that their sides on the equilateral triangle (AB, AC, BC) are each 10, and the other two sides correspond to the segments between the interior points. Possibly, under a 120° rotation, AB maps to AC or BC. But if triangles are congruent, then sides corresponding to the equilateral sides must be equal. Since all equilateral sides are 10, that forces that the sides of the triangles that lie on the equilateral sides are all 10. So indeed, AB, AC, BC are 10 for each triangle? Actually careful: In △BDA, the side on the equilateral triangle is AB = 10. In △AEC, the side on the equilateral triangle is AC = 10. In △CFB, the side on the equilateral triangle is BC = 10. So these three sides are all 10. That automatically matches. So the other two sides of each triangle must match. For example, in △BDA, the other two sides are BD and DA. They must match with the other two sides of △AEC and △CFB. So we have three triangles: ΔBDA: sides (BD, DA, 10); ΔAEC: sides (AE, EC, 10); ΔCFB: sides (CF, FB, 10). Since they are congruent, the sets {BD, DA} and {AE, EC} are equal as sets, and also equal to {CF, FB}. So BD, DA, AE, EC, CF, FB are all equal to each other in some pairing. That means that among BD, DA, AE, EC, CF, FB, there are three distinct numbers if the triangles are not isosceles: two sides equal and one different, or all sides equal. But since they are congruent, it\'s possible that each triangle has two equal sides (isosceles) or all sides different. However, given the symmetry of the equilateral triangle, it is plausible that the triangles are isosceles, but not necessarily.\n\nNow, we have additional constraints from the geometry: Points D, E, F are on segments: D on AE, E on CF, F on BD. Also, DE = 6. And the triangles are inside an equilateral triangle of side 10. We need to determine the perimeter of ΔBDA = BD + DA + 10.\n\nWe should try to set up coordinates or use geometry to relate the lengths.\n\nLet\'s place triangle ABC in the coordinate plane for convenience. Let A = (0,0), B = (10,0), and C = (5, 5√3) (since equilateral triangle side 10). Then the vertices are standard. Now we need to locate points D, E, F. We know D is on AE, E is on CF, F is on BD. Also triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB are congruent. And DE = 6.\n\nWe need to interpret the congruence conditions. Since the triangles are congruent, their corresponding angles are equal. Possibly the triangles are all similar to each other, because all equilateral triangles have angles 60°, so any triangle formed inside could be similar if its angles are 60°. But they are given as congruent, so they are identical. So each triangle has the same set of side lengths and angles.\n\nWe don\'t know the angles of these triangles. But maybe we can find them from the configuration.\n\nLet\'s denote the sides of ΔBDA: Let BD = a, DA = b, AB = 10. So perimeter = a+b+10.\n\nSimilarly, for ΔAEC: AE = ?, EC = ?, AC = 10.\nFor ΔCFB: CF = ?, FB = ?, BC = 10.\n\nCongruence means that (a, b, 10) and (AE, EC, 10) are the same set of numbers, and (a, b, 10) and (CF, FB, 10) are the same set. So there are possibilities: \nCase 1: a = AE, b = EC, and 10 = 10. \nCase 2: a = EC, b = AE.\nCase 3: a = CF, b = FB.\nCase 4: a = FB, b = CF.\nSince the triangles are congruent, it must be that the sets are exactly the same. So either:\n(i) AE = a, EC = b, and CF = a, FB = b.\n(ii) AE = b, EC = a, and CF = b, FB = a.\n(iii) Some other combinations? Possibly AE = a, EC = CF? That would not be consistent because then CF and FB would have to match the remaining side. But since the triangles have three sides, if AE = a and EC = CF, then CF = b and FB = EC = a? That would give a different pattern. But that essentially covers both cases above. So we have two main cases: either the sides of ΔAEC are a and b in some order, and similarly for ΔCFB, or they are swapped.\n\nWe also have relationships from the point placements:\nD is on AE, so AE = AD + DE. AD is the segment from A to D along AE. In ΔBDA, DA = AD, so AD = b. Therefore, AE = b + 6.\nE is on CF, so CF = CE + EF. But we don\'t know CE or EF.\nF is on BD, so BD = BF + FD.\n\nAlso, from congruence, we have AE = either a or b. So if case (i) holds: AE = a, EC = b, then a = b + 6 (from AE = AD+DE = b+6). So a = b+6.\nIf case (ii) holds: AE = b, EC = a, then b = b+6? That gives 0=6, impossible unless b is not AD? But AD = DA = b, so AE = b+6. So AE = b+6, but AE = b would imply b = b+6, contradiction. So case (ii) is impossible. Therefore, we must have case (i): AE = BD = a, EC = DA = b. And similarly, from congruence with ΔCFB, we must have CF = a and FB = b (or possibly the opposite, but likely consistent). So we conclude:\nAE = a = BD,\nEC = b = DA,\nCF = a = BD,\nFB = b = DA.\nThus:\nAE = BD = a,\nEC = DA = b,\nCF = BD = a,\nFB = DA = b.\nSo from AE = AD + DE = b + 6, we get a = b + 6.\nAlso, CF = CE + EF = a.\nBD = BF + FD = a.\nEC = ? But EC = b.\n\nNow we have a = b+6. We need to find a and b.\n\nWe also have other geometric constraints. The triangles are inside an equilateral triangle of side 10, and the segments AE, BD, CF are inside. They intersect at points D, E, F? Actually, D is on AE and BD, but not necessarily the intersection of AE and BD? Wait: D is on AE and also on BD (since BD goes from B to D, D is endpoint). But also D is on AE, so AE passes through D. And F is on BD, so BD passes through F. And E is on CF, so CF passes through E. So the segments AE, BD, CF are not necessarily concurrent; they likely form a triangle DEF inside? But they share points: AE contains D and E? Actually, AE contains D and E? No: AE contains A and E, and D is on AE, so both D and E are on AE? That would mean AE is a segment with endpoints A and E, and D is a point on it. But then D and E are both on AE, so AE is divided into two segments: AD and DE. But E is also on CF. So E is an endpoint of CF? Wait: CF is segment from C to F, and E is on CF, so E is an interior point of CF (unless E is endpoint of CF, but CF ends at F, so E is between C and F or between F and C). So E is on CF. So D and E are both on AE. That means AE and CF intersect at E? Yes, because E is on both AE and CF. So AE and CF intersect at E. Similarly, AE and BD intersect at D? But D is on BD, and D is on AE, so AE and BD intersect at D. BD and CF intersect at F? F is on BD and CF, so BD and CF intersect at F. So indeed, the three segments AE, BD, CF pairwise intersect at D, E, F. So they form a triangle with vertices D, E, F. So D, E, F are the intersections of these three segments inside triangle ABC. So we have triangle DEF inside.\n\nGiven that, we can use coordinates to determine positions of D, E, F and impose conditions that triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB are congruent.\n\nLet\'s assign coordinates:\nA = (0,0)\nB = (10,0)\nC = (5, 5√3) (since altitude = 10*sin60 = 5√3).\n\nWe need to locate D, E, F. They are on segments AE, BD, CF respectively. Also, D is on AE and BD, E is on AE and CF, F is on BD and CF.\n\nLet\'s parameterize:\nSince D is on AE, we can parameterize AE. But we don\'t know E yet. Similarly, E is on CF, and F is on BD.\nWe also have conditions from congruence.\n\nWe have derived: a = BD, b = DA, and a = b+6.\nAlso, AE = a = b+6, so AE = DA + DE.\nEC = b.\nCF = a.\nBD = a.\nFB = b.\n\nWe can try to express coordinates in terms of a and b, and use the fact that triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB are congruent. Congruence gives not only side length equality but also angle equality. Since all triangles are inside an equilateral triangle, they might be oriented similarly. Possibly they are all oriented the same way, or maybe they are rotated copies.\n\nGiven the symmetry suspicion, we might assume that the triangles are arranged symmetrically, meaning that lines AE, BD, CF are such that the triangles are images under 120° rotations. If that is the case, then we would have relationships like: AD = CE? or something? But from our side equalities: AD = b, EC = b, so AD = EC. That matches. Also, BD = CF = a, and DA = FB = b. So that is consistent with a rotation that sends triangle BDA to triangle AEC? Let\'s check: Under a 120° rotation that sends A→B→C→A (counterclockwise or clockwise), does it preserve side lengths? If we want ΔBDA to rotate to ΔAEC, we need BD to become AE, and DA to become EC, and AB to become AC. That would imply BD = AE, DA = EC, AB = AC. We already have that. So if we assume such a rotation exists, then the configuration is symmetric. But is it necessary? Possibly the conditions force that symmetry. So we can try to impose a rotational symmetry.\n\nAssume the entire configuration is symmetric under a 120° rotation about the center of triangle ABC. That is a natural guess given the problem\'s symmetry. Then under a rotation by 120° clockwise about the center O (which is at (5, 5√3/3) if equilateral triangle? Actually, centroid = ( (0+10+5)/3, (0+0+5√3)/3 ) = (15/3, 5√3/3) = (5, 5√3/3) ), the points A, B, C permute. And triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB would be images if the points D, E, F are permuted. That means D, E, F are permuted under rotation. Specifically, if we rotate clockwise, A→B, B→C, C→A, D → D\' (on BD? Actually careful: If we rotate the segment AE, it becomes a segment from B to E\'? Under symmetry, AE rotates to BD, so the image of E is on BD. So if E\' = F (or something), then E rotates to F. Similarly, AE rotates to BD, so E must rotate to a point on BD. The only point on BD that could be E\' is D? But D is on BD? Actually, DD\' would have to be different from D unless D is the center. So likely, E rotates to the intersection of AE and BD? But AE and BD intersect at D, so maybe E rotates to D. That would mean under a 120° rotation, E goes to D. Similarly, D goes to F, and F goes to E. That gives a 3-cycle: E→D, D→F, F→E. Alternatively, maybe D→E, E→F, F→D. We\'ll determine from equations.\n\nIf we assume rotational symmetry of the entire configuration (the whole figure including the triangle ABC and the segments AE, BD, CF), then the rotations map points to points. That implies that distances from vertices to these points are preserved under rotation. For example, distance from A to D equals distance from B to E? Because under rotation, A goes to B, D goes to D\' (say D). So AD = BD. But we already have AD = b and BD = a, so that gives a = b. But we also have a = b+6, so that would give a = b+6 and a = b, so 6=0 contradiction. So rotational symmetry that sends A→B sends D to some point, and we would expect AD = BD if A goes to B and D goes to D? Actually, if A→B and D→E, then AD = AE, not necessarily BD. So it depends on the permutation.\n\nLet\'s consider the possible permutations that preserve congruence. If the triangles are congruent via a 120° rotation, then the correspondence of vertices might be: ΔBDA (vertices B, D, A) maps to ΔAEC (vertices A, E, C) under rotation. That would require: B→A, D→E, A→C. Under a clockwise rotation: A→B, B→C, C→A. So that mapping would send B→C, not A; D→D\', A→B, not C. So that doesn\'t work. Under counterclockwise: A→B, B→C, C→A. To get B→A, we need to map B to the preimage of A under counterclockwise: A→B, so B is the preimage of A? Actually, counterclockwise sends A→B, so the preimage of B under counterclockwise is A. So to have B→A under the same rotation, we need the rotation to be applied to the triangle such that B goes to the vertex that corresponds to A in the target. That means if we rotate the whole triangle BDA counterclockwise by 120°, we get a triangle with vertices: B^{(counterclockwise)} → C, D^{(counterclockwise)} → D\', A^{(counterclockwise)} → B. So that triangle is C, D\', B, which is triangle CFB if D\' = F. So that maps ΔBDA to ΔCFB. To map to ΔAEC, we would need a rotation that sends B to A, D to E, A to C. That could be a rotation by 120° around a different center? But the entire figure is likely symmetric under the equilateral triangle\'s symmetry, which is rotation by 120° about its center. So if the triangles are congruent and the configuration is symmetric, the mapping from one triangle to another should be a symmetry of ABC. So the only symmetries of ABC are rotations of 120° and reflections. So the mapping must be a rotation by 120° or possibly a reflection, but unlikely. So we can try both.\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe we can determine a and b using geometry and the given DE=6 without assuming the full rotational symmetry.\n\nLet\'s try to set up coordinates. Let’s denote coordinates for D, E, F.\n\nWe have:\nA = (0,0)\nB = (10,0)\nC = (5,5√3)\n\nD is on AE, so D lies on line from A to E. We don\'t know E yet.\nE is on CF, so E lies on line from C to F.\nF is on BD, so F lies on line from B to D.\n\nAlso, we have lengths: AD = b, AE = a = b+6, so DE = 6.\nBD = a, DA = b, so we know lengths of these segments.\n\nWe need to impose that triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB are congruent.Congruence means that all corresponding sides and angles are equal. Possibly we can use side lengths as we did, and also maybe angles. Since triangle ABC is equilateral, the sides of these triangles are inside, and they might have specific shapes.\n\nMaybe we can find angles of these triangles. Notice that triangle ABC is equilateral, so angles at each vertex are 60°. The points D, E, F are inside, so triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB share a side with ABC. So angle at B in ΔBDA is part of angle B of triangle ABC? Actually, angle at B in triangle ABC is 60°, and part of that is angle ABD? Not necessarily. But we can consider the angles at vertices A, B, C for these triangles.\n\nLet\'s denote angles in ΔBDA: Let ∠BDA = α, ∠DAB = β, ∠ABD = γ. Since it\'s a triangle, α+β+γ=180°.\n\nIn ΔAEC: vertices A, E, C. Its angles: ∠AEC = δ, ∠EAC = ε, ∠ACE = ζ.\nIn ΔCFB: vertices C, F, B. Its angles: ∠CFB = η, ∠FCB = θ, ∠FBC = ι.\n\nCongruence means that these triangles have the same angles. So α=δ, β=ε, γ=ζ? Or some other correspondence. Without knowing the correspondence, we can try to use the fact that these triangles are placed inside an equilateral triangle, and they might have angles that are multiples of 60° or complementary.\n\nMaybe we can use law of cosines in triangles involving the equilateral triangle sides.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to express coordinates of D, E, F in terms of parameters and use the length conditions.\n\nLet\'s try to set up coordinates for D, E, F.\n\nFirst, we know AD = b, and DE = 6, so AE = b+6.\nSo D lies on AE at distance b from A, and E lies on AE at distance b+6 from A. So if we parameterize AE as a line from A to E, then D is at distance b along that line, and E is at distance b+6 along that line. But we don\'t know the direction of AE.\n\nSimilarly, BD = a, and we know BD is a segment from B to D. So D lies on BD at distance something from B? Actually, BD = a, so distance from B to D is a.\n\nAlso, CF = a, and F lies on CF such that CF = a.\n\nAnd FB = b, so distance from F to B is b.\nEC = b, so distance from E to C is b.\n\nWe have many conditions: D is on AE and BD, E is on AE and CF, F is on BD and CF.\n\nWe can try to assign coordinates to D, E, F and use the distances.\n\nLet’s denote:\nD = (x_D, y_D)\nE = (x_E, y_E)\nF = (x_F, y_F)\n\nConstraints:\n1. D lies on segment AE: A=(0,0), E=(x_E, y_E). So D is on line AE, and distance AD = b, DE = 6. So \n   (x_D, y_D) = (t*x_E, t*y_E) for some t, and t*b = b, (1-t)*6? Actually, if AD = b and DE = 6, then since AE = b+6, and D is between A and E (likely, because D is on AE and E is further from A?), then t = b/(b+6). So \n   x_D = (b/(b+6)) x_E, y_D = (b/(b+6)) y_E.\n2. E lies on segment CF: C=(5,5√3), F=(x_F, y_F). So E is on line CF, and distance CE = b, EF = a - b? Actually, CF = a, and E is on CF, so CE + EF = a. But we also have CE = b? From congruence, EC = b. So indeed CE = b. Then EF = a - b = (b+6) - b = 6? Because a = b+6, so EF = (b+6) - b = 6. So interestingly, EF = 6 as well. So AE = b+6, DE = 6, EF = 6. So lengths along the boundaries are symmetric? Possibly.\n   So if we parameterize CF from C to F, and E is at distance b from C, then the total CF = a = b+6, so the remaining to F is also 6. So E divides CF into segments CE = b and EF = 6. So if we let u = b/(b+6), then E = C + u*(F-C), so that CE = b. Alternatively, if we parameterize from F to C, we could also say E = (1-u)*(C) + u*(F)? Actually, if we go from C to F, distance b corresponds to parameter t = b/(b+6). So \n   x_E = (1 - u?) Let\'s do: \n   Let F = (x_F, y_F). Then E = (C + t*(F-C))? Actually, if E is between C and F, then E = C + (b/a)*(F-C) if we go from C to F. Since a = b+6, t = b/(b+6). So \n   x_E = 5 + b/(b+6) (x_F - 5), \n   y_E = 5√3 + b/(b+6) (y_F - 5√3).\n   But note: we also have D on CF? D is on AE and BD, not on CF except at E? Actually, D is not on CF. So D is not necessarily on CF.\n3. F lies on BD: B=(10,0), D=(x_D, y_D). So F is on line BD, and BD = a, and FB = b. So F divides BD such that BF = b and FD = b+6? Because BD = a = b+6, and FB = b, so FD = a - b = 6. So if we parameterize BD from B to D, distance b corresponds to parameter t = b/(b+6). So \n   x_F = 10 + b/(b+6) (x_D - 10),\n   y_F = 0 + b/(b+6) (y_D - 0).\n\nNow we have three equations expressing E in terms of F (from CF), and also F in terms of D (from BD), and D in terms of E (from AE). But we also need that E is on CF as defined, and D is on BD as defined. These equations should be consistent. They give relationships between coordinates.\n\nWe also have the condition that triangles ΔBDA, ΔAEC, ΔCFB are congruent. We have already used side lengths from that to deduce a = b+6 and the other equalities. But we haven\'t used any condition about angles or the fact that triangles are congruent as whole triangles (maybe orientation matters). Possibly we need to use that the triangles share the same angles, which might impose conditions on the directions.\n\nFrom side lengths, we have: \nAD = b, BD = a, so in triangle ABD, we have sides AB=10, AD=b, BD=a. \nAlso, in triangle ADC? Not needed.\nBut we also have triangle AEC: AE = a, EC = b, AC=10.\nAnd triangle CFB: CF = a, FB = b, BC=10.\nSo these triangles have the same side lengths. That means they are congruent if the correspondence is such that sides match. We have matched: In ΔBDA, sides: AB=10, AD=b, BD=a. In ΔAEC, sides: AC=10, AE=a, EC=b. In ΔCFB, sides: BC=10, CF=a, FB=b. So they have the same three numbers: 10, a, b. So they are congruent provided that a and b are the same for all, which they are. So the side length conditions alone make them congruent. So the congruence condition is satisfied as long as the side lengths are as we deduced. But wait: That deduction assumed that