roach: Use law of cosines in triangles involving the equilateral triangle sides. Consider triangle ABD: sides AB=10, AD=b, BD=a. We know a = b+6. We can compute angle ABD using law of cosines. Also, triangle ACE: sides AC=10, AD=b, AE=b+6? Actually careful: triangle AEC has vertices A, E, C. So sides: AC=10, AE=b+6, EC=b. So we have triangle AEC with sides 10, b+6, b. That triangle is congruent to triangle BDA (10, b, b+6). So they have the same side lengths. That means their angles are the same. So angle BAC in triangle BDA corresponds to angle EAC in triangle AEC? Actually, in triangle BDA, angle at A is ∠BAD. In triangle AEC, angle at A is ∠CAE. These angles might not be equal because triangles are congruent but not necessarily located in the same orientation. However, congruence means all angles are equal, so ∠BAD = ∠CAE (if correspondence is A→A, B→E, D→C? Actually, if triangles are congruent with correspondence: B→A, D→E, A→C in triangle BDA to triangle AEC? That would give ∠ABD = ∠CAE, ∠BAD = ∠AEC, ∠BDA = ∠ACE. That seems messy.\n\nMaybe we can set up equations using coordinates and the fact that D, E, F are inside ABC. That might give conditions on b.\n\nLet\'s proceed with coordinates.\n\nWe have:\nA = (0,0)\nB = (10,0)\nC = (5,5√3)\n\nWe introduce parameters b and a = b+6.\n\nLet D = (x_D, y_D)\nSince D is on AE and BD, and AD = b, BD = a.\n\nFirst, D lies on BD. BD is segment from B to D. So D -> F on BD? Actually, F is on BD. But D is an endpoint of BD. So BD = a, and F lies on BD such that BF = b. That means F is between B and D, and BF = b, FD = a-b = 6. So we can parameterize BD: \nF = B + (b/a)*(D - B) = (10,0) + (b/(b+6))*(x_D - 10, y_D).\nSo F = (10 + b/(b+6)(x_D-10), b/(b+6) y_D).\n\nSecond, E lies on CF and AE.\nE is on AE and CF.\nOn AE: E = D + 6*(unit vector from D to E)? Actually, DE = 6, and D is between A and E (since AE = b+6, AD = b, so DE=6). So E = D + 6*(unit vector from D to E in the direction from A to E). But we don\'t know the dire