e tuple. That means new tuple = (A(1), A(2), A(3), A(4))? But careful: A(1)=3, A(2)=4, A(3)=1, A(4)=2. So the tuple becomes (3,4,1,2). So if we think of the permutation as acting on the values, it is exactly that: the permutation applied to the numbers. Alternatively, if we think of it as acting on the indices (the registers), it would be a different permutation. Actually it\'s consistent: the permutation we apply to the tuple is exactly the permutation that sends the old value in register i to the new value in register? Wait: The tuple specifies which number is in which register. The operation rearranges the numbers. So if we let the permutation P be the permutation on the set of numbers (they are the elements), then after applying P, the new assignment is: R_i gets P(R_i)? That would not work because R_i is an index. Better: We can think of the state as an ordered list (the numbers). When we apply a permutation on the positions of the list, we are rearranging the order. But our operation A swaps positions 1 and 3, and swaps positions 2 and 4. That corresponds to applying the permutation (1,3)(2,4) to the indices? Actually if we think of the tuple as being a list, applying a permutation to the indices means: new tuple at position i is the old tuple at position σ(i). So if we apply σ = (1,3)(2,4), then new tuple = (old tuple(3), old tuple(4), old tuple(1), old tuple(2)) = (3,4,1,2) if old tuple is (1,2,3,4). So indeed, the permutation applies to the indices. So if we start with the identity permutation (where tuple at position i is i), applying σ gives (σ(1), σ(2), σ(3), σ(4)) = (3,4,1,2). That is exactly the result. So we can think of the initial tuple as the identity permutation from indices to values. Then applying A gives the permutation ρ = (1,3)(2,4) (as a permutation on the indices). But careful: The permutation on the indices is what changes the mapping from indices to values. So after applying ρ, the new tuple is (ρ(1), ρ(2), ρ(3), ρ(4)). So if we represent