to determine k from geometry.\n\nWe know ABCD has sides: AB=7, AD=2, angle 120°. So its sides are 7 and 2. In ECFA, let its sides be: side along direction of AB: let its length be x; and side along direction of AD (120°): let its length be y. Since it is similar, these correspond to the sides 7 and 2, so either x/7 = y/2 = k, or possibly reversed, but typically corresponding sides are proportional. But careful: In parallelogram ECFA, the sides adjacent to A are EA and AF. Which one corresponds to AB and which to AD? That depends on the placement. But we assume similarity means the ratios of sides are the same, so x/y = 7/2 or 2/7 depending on correspondence. But in ABCD, the sides adjacent to A are of lengths AB=7 and AD=2, so the ratio is either 7/2 or 2/7. So in ECFA, the ratio of lengths of sides from A should also be either 7/2 or 2/7. But we don\'t know which vertex corresponds to which.\n\nGiven that A is a vertex in both, it\'s plausible that the sides from A in ECFA correspond to the sides from A in ABCD. So if AE is along AB and AF is along AD, then AE and AF are in the same directions as AB and AD. But we already saw that gives E and F as B and D, leading to the whole parallelogram. So that\'s not interior. So maybe AE corresponds to AD and AF to AB? That we also saw gives the whole parallelogram. So likely, E and F are not on the sides from A; they are on other sides. Then the sides AE and AF are not along the sides from A; they are inside. But their directions could be along the sides? Possibly AE is along AD direction but not necessarily from A; AE could be a segment from A to a point on BC or CD? That segment would not be parallel to AD unless the point is on AD. So maybe AE is parallel to something? For parallelogram ECFA, the sides are parallel to those of ABCD. So if ECFA is similar and also aligned (same orientation), then its sides are parallel to sides of ABCD. That means AE is parallel to either AB or AD, and EC is parallel to either AB or AD. Since AE is a side from A, if it is parallel to AB, then it is horizontal; if parallel to AD, then it is at 120°. Similarly, EC from E to C: if it is parallel to AB, then EC is horizontal; if parallel to AD, then EC is at 120°. And since ABCD has sides AB (horizontal) and AD (120°), if ECFA has sides parallel to these, then it is exactly oriented the same as ABCD. So in that case, all sides of ECFA are either horizontal or at 120°. So angles at A and C are 120° because horizontal and 120° path between them give 120°? Actually if one side is horizontal and the other is at 120°, the angle between them is 120° indeed. So that aligns.\n\nThus, assuming ECFA has sides parallel to those of ABCD, we have two possibilities for orientation at A: either AE is horizontal and AF is at 120°, or AE is at 120° and AF is horizontal. But as we computed, both give E and F at the vertices of ABCD when AE+AF adds to AC. So if we want EACS to be an interior parallelogram, then AE and AF cannot both go to vertices along the sides from A. So one of them must go to a point on BC or CD. That means that AE or AF is not starting from A along the side of ABCD, but rather from A to a point on the opposite side. For example, if AE is horizontal, then E is on a horizontal line from A. That horizontal line could intersect side BC or CD? A is at (0,0), horizontal line y=0 intersects side BC from (7,0) to (6,√3)? Actually y=0 intersects line BC? BC goes from (7,0) to (6,√3), so on BC, y varies from 0 to √3, so y=0 only at (7,0) which is B, so horizontal from A only hits B. It also intersects CD? CD is from (6,√3) to (-1,√3), so horizontal line y=0 does not intersect CD. It intersects AD? AD goes from (0,0) to (-1,√3), so horizontal from A is along AB only, because AD is not horizontal. So if AE is horizontal, E must be on AB, and AB is only segment from (0,0) to (7,0). So E is on AB. But then if AE is horizontal, AE is along AB, and we already considered that and it gave trivial solution unless we allow E to be between A and B? But if AE is a segment from A to a point on AB, then E is on AB, and AE is along AB. That is the horizontal direction. So if AE is horizontal, then E is on AB. Similarly, if AF is at 120°, then AF is along AD? Because AD is at 120° from A. So AF along AD means F is on AD. That again gives the trivial solution. So if we want a nontrivial interior parallelogram, we cannot have both AE along AB and AF along AD. Therefore, ECFA cannot have sides parallel to ABCD if we require it to be interior and share vertices A and C? Wait, maybe ECPA is not oriented exactly the same as ABCD, but rotated by 60°? That would preserve angles but change orientation. For example, at A, if AE is at 60° and AF is at 0°, then angle between them is 60°, not 120°. So that would not match the 120° angle at A in ABCD. To have angle 120° at A, the sides must make an angle of 120°. So if one side is horizontal, the other must be at 120° (or 60°? Actually if one is horizontal, the other could be at 120° or at 60° to get 120°? The angle between a horizontal and a line at 60° below horizontal is 60°? Actually if line is at 60° from horizontal, the angle between them is 60°. So to get 120°, the other line must be at 120° from horizontal, or at -60° (i.e., 60° below horizontal in the opposite direction)? If horizontal is at 0°, a line at 120° (60° clockwise from horizontal?) Actually 120° is 60° past 90°, so it\'s 120° from horizontal, that gives an angle of 120°. If a line is at -60° (which is 60° below horizontal to the right), the angle between 0° and -60° is 60°. So only 120° and 60° are supplementary. So to get 120°, one line must be at 0° and the other at 120°, or one at 60° and the other at 180°? That would be 120° as well? Actually angle between 60° and 180° is 120°. So possibilities: (0°,120°) or (60°,180°) or (120°,60°) or (180°,120°). So there are combinations that yield 120°. So ECFA could have sides at, say, 60° and 180° relative to horizontal. That would also give a 120° angle at A. But then those directions are not the same as those of ABCD? ABCD has sides horizontal and at 120°. So if ECFA uses directions 60° and 180°, that is rotated by -30° relative to ABCD? That would change orientation but still yield 120° angle. But then sides would not be parallel to ABCD\'s sides. That is possible. So maybe ECFA is rotated by 30° relative to ABCD? Let\'s check: If one side is at 60° and the other at 180°, the angle between them is 120°. But 60° is not the same as 120°, but 60° is complementary in some sense? Actually 60° is half of 120°. But I doubt that.\n\nGiven the problem is likely from an olympiad or contest, there is probably a neat answer. Let\'s search memory: There is a known problem: "In parallelogram ABCD with sides 7 and 2 and angle 120°, parallelogram ECFA inside it is similar. Find area ratio." Maybe the answer is 1/4 or something.\n\nWe need to determine the configuration systematically.\n\nLet\'s denote parallelogram ABCD. We have its area = 7*2*sin120° = 14*√3/2 = 7√3.\nNow, parallelogram ECFA is similar, so its sides are in proportion. Let the sides of ECFA be m and n, where m and n are the side lengths. Since similarity, m/n = 7/2 or n/m = 7/2. But we don\'t know correspondence.\n\nWe also know that ECFA is contained in ABCD and shares vertices A and C. So its diagonal AC is common. In parallelogram ECFA, the diagonal AC and the side lengths m and n are related. For a parallelogram, the diagonals are given by formulas: If sides are a and b, and one angle is θ, then the diagonals are √(a^2+b^2+2ab cosθ) and √(a^2+b^2-2ab cosθ). In ABCD, diagonal AC = √(7^2+2^2+2*7*2*cos120°) = √(49+4+28*(-1/2)) = √(49+4-14) = √(39). Similarly, diagonal BD = √(49+4-14)=√(39) as well? Actually cos120°=-1/2, so both diagonals? Actually, sides 7 and 2, angle 120°, so one diagonal (from A to C) uses sides AB and AD with angle 120°, so d1 = √(7^2+2^2+2*7*2*cos120°) = √(49+4-14)=√39. The other diagonal (from B to D) uses sides AB and 